We are given a math club with 20 members. We want to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer such that no member can hold more than one office.  
We need to find the number of ways to do this.  
We can choose the president from the 20 members. After choosing the president, there are 19 members remaining.  
We can choose the vice-president from the remaining 19 members. After choosing the president and vice-president, there are 18 members remaining.  
We can choose the treasurer from the remaining 18 members.  
The total number of ways to select a president, vice-president, and treasurer is the number of permutations of 20 members taken 3 at a time, which is denoted as $P(20,3)$ or $_{20}P_3$.
The formula for permutations is $P(n,r) = \frac{n!}{(n-r)!}$. In this case, $n=20$ and $r=3$, so we have
$P(20,3) = \frac{20!}{(20-3)!} = \frac{20!}{17!} = 20 \times 19 \times 18$.
